9. Loops:

    9.a for loops:

        The for Loop bring the initialization, condition, and increment/decrement updates into a single line at the header

            for (initialization; condition; update)
            {
                // code to be executed
            }

        Example:

            #include <stdio.h>

            int main()
            {
       		    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
        		printf("%d ", i);
            }
            return (0);
            }

            // Output: 1 2 3 4 5

                Initialization (int i = 1:) Sets up a loop counter variable. Runs exactly once.
                Condition (i <= 5:) Evaluated before iteration. If true, the loop body runs; if false, the loop terminates.
        		Update (i++:) Changes the counter variable after the loop body finishes executing.


    9.b while loops:

        The while Loop continuously executes a block of code as Long as its specified conditions to true.
        You must manually firstize your variable before the loop and modify it inside the body.

            initialization;
            while (condition)
            {
                // code to be executed
                update;
        	}

            Example:

                #include <stdio.h>

                int main()
                {
                    int i;

                    i = 1;  // Initialization
                    while (i <= 5)  // Condition
                    {
            		    printf("%d ", i);
                        i++;            // Update inside the loop body
                    }
                return (0);
                }
                        // Output: 1 2 3 4 5

            Warning:    If you forget to include the update (i++), the condition will never become false,
                        creating an Infinite loop That crashes or hangs your program.


    9.c do-while loop:

        The do-while loop is an exit-controlled loop.
        It guarantees that the code block will execute at least once Before evaluating the conditional test expression.
        Note the mandatory semicolon ; After the while statement.

            initialization;
            do
            {
                // code to be executed
                update;
            }
            while (condition);

        Example:

            #include <stdio.h>

            int main()
            {
            int i;

            i = 10;
            do
            {
                printf("This will print exactly once.");
                i++;
            }
            while (i < 5);  // Condition is false because 11 is not less than 5
            return (0);
            }


9.d If-else:

    An if-else statement inside a loop is one of the most powerful combinations in C programming.
    It allows Your program to repeat an action multiple times (using the loop)
    and make a specific decision during each individual repetition (using if-else).


        Typical use cases:

            Filtering data: Search or skip certain values in an array.
            Categorize: Share data (e.g. straight/outre numbers, positive/negative values).
            Loop control: Breaking the loop prematurely (break) or jump to the next pass (continue) if a condition is fulfilled.


9.e Nested Loop:

    A nested while- Loop (nested while loop) simply means that
    a while- Loop within another while- Loop is located.

    The most important principle here is:
    For each individual run of the outer loop,
    the inner loop runs completely from beginning to end through.

        A clean C example, the one 3x2 matrix (coordinate grid) Simulated:

            #include <stdio.h>

            int main()
            {
            int i;

            1 = 0;                      // 1. Initialion of the outer loop

        	while (i < 3)               // 2. Condition of the outer loop
            {
            	int j = 0;              // 3. Important: The varible will be reset in every outer loop

            	while (j < 2)           // 4. Condition of the inner loop
            	{
            		printf("i: %d, j: %d\n", i, j);
                	j++;         		    // 5. Incrementation of the inner loop
            	}
                i++;                    // 6. Incrementation of the outer loop
                }
            return (0);
            }

    Common source of error in (while):

        The biggest pitfall is the line int j = 0;.
        Put j outside the external while- Loop instead of right in front of it,
        the inner runs loop only once (at the very first run of i).
        After that stays j on the maximum value and the internal condition never comes true again.


9.f Loop Control Statements:

    C gives you keywords to old the execution flow of your loops dynamically:

        break: Immediately terminates the loop and jumps to the code following it

        continue: Skips the rest of the current iteration's code block and forces the loop to evaluate the next cycle.

            Example:

                for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++)
                {
                    if (i == 2) continue;           // Skips printing 2
            	    if (i == 4) break;              // Stops the loop completely when i hits 4
                    printf("%d ", i);
                }
                	// Output: 1 3


9.g Ternary(?):

    The Ternary Operator (also called a condition operator) is a compact short-term spelling for a simple if-else- Instruction.
    It is the only operator in C, the three operands (parts) needed.

        Varible = (Condition) ? expressionTrue : expressionFalse;

            Condition:			An expression that is either true or false.
            expressionTrue:		This value is chose if the condition applies.
            expressionFalse:	This value is selected if the condition is false.

                Example classic if-else:

                    int a;
                    int b;
                	int max;

                    a = 10;
                    b = 20;

                    if (a > b)
                    {
                        max = a;
					}
                    else
                    {
                        max = b;
                    }

                Example with ternary operator:

                    int a;
                    int b;

                    a = 10;
                    b = 20;

                    int max = (a > b) ? a : b;
